+// amxts: regular expressions as JavaScript has them - `/(\w+)@(\w+)/i`,
+// `new RegExp(text, "g")`, test, exec, and String's match, matchAll,
+// replace, replaceAll, search and split with one (the compiler sends those
+// here, Resolver.objectCallOf).
+//
+// A pattern is parsed into a tree, compiled to a small program and run by a
+// backtracking matcher over UTF-16 code units, as JavaScript matches without
+// the `u` flag. It has characters and escapes (\d \w \s \b and their
+// negations, \n \t \xHH \uHHHH), `.`, classes, anchors, groups - capturing,
+// `(?:)`, lookahead `(?=)` `(?!)` - alternation, greedy and lazy quantifiers
+// and back-references; the flags g, i, m, s and y. Not here: lookbehind,
+// named groups, `\p{...}` and the `u` and `d` flags - a pattern with one is a
+// SyntaxError. A match that takes more than a few million steps throws an
+// Error rather than hold the server.
